module sh.sysl.ogol

import sh.sysl.parsing.source_of

// The read-run-print loop, which is the same on every machine Ogol runs on.
//
// **It lives in the language rather than in each console, because there is more than one console and
// there will be more.** There are two today — a desktop terminal and a Pico 2 W over a serial cable —
// and an STM32 is coming, and the Pico's other core is a different target for the same board. Written
// out per platform, this loop would be four copies of twenty lines that drift: the one someone uses
// daily grows a fix, the others do not, and the difference then reads as a *board* bug rather than as
// a console that was never updated.
//
// **`*Iterate[string]` is what makes one loop serve all of them.** A `sysl.term.edit.Editor` and a
// `sysl.io.Lines` are both one, so the platform decides which to build and nothing below that line
// knows the difference. What is left for a console to do is name its streams and print its own
// banner — a dozen lines, none of it about Ogol.
//
// **The interpreter is a parameter rather than made here**, and that is what makes this testable: a
// test hands it a cursor over a byte script and reads `transcript` afterwards. A `session` that made
// its own interpreter would leave a test nothing to look at, which is how a loop like this ends up
// being the one part of a language that nothing checks.

// Every line the cursor yields, run, with what it came to printed.
//
// Returns when the cursor ends or when `bye` is typed on a line of its own. **What to do about that is
// the caller's**, and the two answers differ: a hosted program returns from `main`, while a board has
// nowhere to exit *to* and says so rather than falling off the end.
//
// The **continuation prompt** is the reason `More` is a case of its own. A line that stops inside a
// `[` or before an `end` has not gone wrong — it is held, `....` is printed, and the next line is
// added to it. Anything else is a refusal, printed and dropped.
//
// **A refusal from the reader is printed against the text it was about**, with the line quoted and a
// caret under the word — which is what the spans on the tokens are for and is worth most in a
// console, where a held definition may be several lines long by the time something in it is refused.
// A refusal from the evaluator has no place to point at and prints as its sentence; `report` answers
// both, so there is one call here rather than a test.
session(i: &Ogol, cursor: *Iterate[string])
    // What has been typed and not yet run, because it was not finished. Empty is the ordinary state.
    var pending = ""

    prints("ogol> ")

    for line in cursor
        if pending == "" && line == "bye" then break

        var src = if pending == "" then line else pending + "\n" + line

        pending = ""

        run(i, src) match
            // A command answers with nothing, and a console that echoed a blank line for every
            // `print` would double every program's output.
            Ok(v) ->
                v match
                    Nothing -> ()
                    _ -> print(render(v))
            Err(f) ->
                f match
                    More -> pending = src
                    _ -> print(report(f, source_of("<console>", src)))

        prints(if pending == "" then "ogol> " else ".... ")

    print("")
end session
